WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.322
Scenario: A regular supplier emails an updated invoice with

2
00:00:03.322 --> 00:00:06.644
new bank details and says payment must be made

3
00:00:06.644 --> 00:00:09.966
today to avoid a penalty. Safest response: Option D:

4
00:00:09.966 --> 00:00:13.289
Verify the change through a separately established phone number

5
00:00:13.289 --> 00:00:16.611
or approved vendor portal and follow dual-approval controls. Payment

6
00:00:16.611 --> 00:00:19.933
changes need independent verification through trusted records and the

7
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:23.255
normal authorization process. Option A is unsafe because The

8
00:00:23.255 --> 00:00:26.577
email may provide an attacker-controlled number; use contact details

9
00:00:26.577 --> 00:00:29.899
already on file. Option B is unsafe because If

10
00:00:29.899 --> 00:00:33.221
the account is compromised, the attacker may confirm their

11
00:00:33.221 --> 00:00:36.544
own fraudulent instructions. Option C is unsafe because A

12
00:00:36.544 --> 00:00:39.866
test payment still sends funds to an unverified beneficiary

13
00:00:39.866 --> 00:00:43.188
and can create loss. In practice: Freeze the change

14
00:00:43.188 --> 00:00:46.510
until independently verified using contact information already on file,

15
00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.832
and apply the organization’s payment authorization controls. Key takeaway:

16
00:00:49.832 --> 00:00:53.154
Never trust changed payment instructions solely because they arrive

17
00:00:53.154 --> 00:00:55.000
in a familiar email thread.
